One common way Java APIs can lead to vulnerabilities is through improper handling of user input when interacting with the system shell, which can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // This code is vulnerable to command injection because it concatenates 
        // untrusted user input directly into a shell command string.
        String command = "ping -c 1 " + userInput;

        // The Runtime.getRuntime().exec(String) method executes the command through the OS shell
        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);

        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class FixedCommandInjectionExample {
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // To prevent injection, we avoid using a single String command.
        // Instead, we use the version of exec that accepts a String array.
        // This treats the user input strictly as an argument, not as part of the command syntax.

        // Validate input to ensure it only contains expected characters (e.g., alphanumeric and dots)
        if (!userInput.matches("^[a-zA-Z0-9.]+$")) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid characters in hostname");
        }

        // The command and its arguments are passed as discrete elements in an array
        String[] command = {"ping", "-c", "1", userInput};

        // This version of exec does not invoke a shell, making it safe from command chaining (e.g., "; rm -rf /")
        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);

        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

